WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.503
Tuvalu has become a global symbol of climate change.

00:00:03.503 --> 00:00:07.989
How Tuvaluans respond to that is a story of politics as much as geography.

00:00:09.089 --> 00:00:12.488
Each island community is governed through its falekaupule,

00:00:12.488 --> 00:00:16.472
a traditional assembly of elders, alongside the national parliament.

00:00:16.472 --> 00:00:18.053
Land is held by kin groups.

00:00:18.053 --> 00:00:22.975
That makes moving people within the country hard, even where land physically exists.

00:00:24.075 --> 00:00:29.110
Culture binds these communities: the fatele, a dance-song form performed at celebrations,

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:31.203
brings each island's people together.

00:00:32.303 --> 00:00:34.642
Tuvalu is responding on several fronts.

00:00:34.642 --> 00:00:39.560
Land is being reclaimed and raised on Fogafale under a coastal adaptation project.

00:00:40.660 --> 00:00:42.489
Satellites can follow the work:

00:00:42.489 --> 00:00:47.092
these Sentinel-2 images show new land built out along Fogafale's lagoon shore.

00:00:48.192 --> 00:00:53.078
In 2023 Tuvalu amended its constitution to state that its statehood will continue

00:00:53.078 --> 00:00:54.828
whatever happens to its land.

00:00:54.828 --> 00:00:58.750
The same year it signed the Falepili Union treaty with Australia,

00:00:58.750 --> 00:01:02.309
which opens a pathway for Tuvaluans to live and work there.

00:01:03.409 --> 00:01:07.237
Every one of these policies responds to a prediction about the land.

00:01:07.237 --> 00:01:10.840
Kench and colleagues ask whether that prediction should be loss.

00:01:10.840 --> 00:01:13.823
And Baldacchino reminds us to ask who sets the terms.
